From 5590ea1f7e819167501d62098469232d695240be Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 14sea <wanhuaning@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 17 May 2026 14:53:44 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Phase 3 NH6: wb_altasmi_parallel power-on reset stretcher for
 altasmi
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 3 mode 'P' STATUS-trace diagnostic (patch 0010 + HW test
2026-05-17 evening, UART capture md5 d9330cae...) showed: Phase A
RDSR (opcode 0x05) completes cleanly in 6 iters, but Phase B READ
(opcode 0x03) sees STATUS=0x03 (busy=1, data_valid=1) the cycle
CTRL=READ is written.  busy_wire stays at 1 across 10M poll iters,
and am_data_valid is level-held (not pulsed) — the wrapper's
flag_data_valid latch re-asserts the cycle after every
dataout_read_req strobe.

Paper audit of wb_altasmi_parallel.v found the root cause: the
megafunction's reset input is driven ONLY by the firmware-issued
ctrl_reset pulse.  At power-up rst_n deasserts → ctrl_reset stays
0 forever (until firmware writes CTRL.b6=1, which never happens
during normal boot).  Per Altera altasmi_parallel documentation,
reset must be asserted ≥250 ns at power-up to initialize the
megafunction's internal FIFO depth counters and FSM.  Without that
init pulse, the FIFO counters power up in an undefined state that
the megafunction interprets as "data is already ready" for
multi-byte-shift opcodes (READ, WRITE) but happens to be ignored
by the single-byte RDSR path — explaining why Phase A worked.

This patch adds a synchronous 31-cycle power-on reset stretcher
gated by a 5-bit saturating counter.  The stretched signal is
ORed with firmware-driven ctrl_reset and fed to epcs_ctrl.reset.
31 cycles @ 50 MHz = 620 ns, 2.5× the spec minimum of 250 ns.

  reg [4:0] am_por_cnt;
  wire am_por_done = &am_por_cnt;
  always @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n) begin
      if (!rst_n)             am_por_cnt <= 5'd0;
      else if (!am_por_done)  am_por_cnt <= am_por_cnt + 5'd1;
  end
  wire am_reset_in = ctrl_reset | ~am_por_done;
  epcs_ctrl u_am ( .reset(am_reset_in), ... );

Resource cost: 5 logic registers + counter increment + OR gate.
Negligible vs current LE 85 %.

Phase 3 closure criterion (pre-HW expectation): Phase B READ
would succeed with this fix — busy clears, data_valid pulses
per-byte, DATAOUT yields real flash content at 0x000000.

POST-HW TEST REFUTED (2026-05-17 evening, bitstream md5
aa41f714cb5675fb2ef616a324ad354): trace md5 d9330cae —
byte-identical to the diagnostic baseline `f931e805` capture
(see docs/captures/diag_baseline_hang.log).  Phase B still hangs
at CTRL=READ pre-amble with STATUS=0x03.  NH6 reset stretcher had
ZERO observable effect on FSM behavior.  Root cause is NOT
"missing POR reset" — turned out to be a structural deadlock in
the qmegawiz output's READ FSM (stage_cntr freezes off 2'b10 in
stage4 because FSM clk_en gates on ~end_read; end_read only
fires at stage 2'b10 → chicken-and-egg).  See plan.md Phase 3
NH8 v3 section + reference-rublock-complexity.md
§"Structural deadlock".

Patch retained as DEFENSIVE: the altasmi datasheet does call for
a ≥250 ns power-up reset.  This stretcher provides it (620 ns
@ 50 MHz) regardless of whether it fixed the current bug.
Removing it would be a spec regression.

Cumulative refuted-list this hypothesis added to: NH1 (firmware
race, prior session) + NH4 (shift_bytes pulse, this diag) + NH5
(chip mismatch, mostly, since RDSR works) + NH6 (this patch).
NH2/NH3/NH7/NH8 also refuted in subsequent patches; all 4
hypotheses landed at the same byte-identical hang signature.
---
 rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v | 22 +++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v b/rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v
index 8df2090..97f1bfb 100644
--- a/rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v
+++ b/rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v
@@ -67,12 +67,32 @@ module wb_altasmi_parallel (
     wire        am_illegal_erase;
     wire        am_illegal_write;
 
+    // ── Power-on reset stretcher (NH6 fix) ────────────────────────────
+    // altasmi_parallel spec: reset must be asserted ≥250 ns at power-up
+    // to initialize the megafunction (FIFO counters, internal FSM).
+    // Without this, am_data_valid is level-held high during pre-amble on
+    // READ-class opcodes — exactly the symptom captured by patch 0010's
+    // diagnostic STATUS-trace (Phase A RDSR works, Phase B READ hangs at
+    // pre-amble with STATUS=0x03 across 10M poll iters).
+    //
+    // Stretch the megafunction's reset for 31 cycles after rst_n
+    // deasserts (= 620 ns @ 50 MHz, 2.5× the spec minimum), then OR
+    // with the firmware-issued ctrl_reset pulse so firmware can still
+    // request a soft-reset on demand.
+    reg [4:0] am_por_cnt;
+    wire am_por_done = &am_por_cnt;  // saturates at 5'b11111
+    always @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n) begin
+        if (!rst_n)             am_por_cnt <= 5'd0;
+        else if (!am_por_done)  am_por_cnt <= am_por_cnt + 5'd1;
+    end
+    wire am_reset_in = ctrl_reset | ~am_por_done;
+
     // Generated by qmegawiz from rtl/ip/epcs_ctrl.v (altasmi_parallel
     // wrapper, EPCS16, Cyclone IV E, USE_ASMIBLOCK=ON — drives the
     // dedicated AS pins DCLK/nCSO/ASDO/DATA0 automatically).
     epcs_ctrl u_am (
         .clkin         (clk),
-        .reset         (ctrl_reset),
+        .reset         (am_reset_in),
         .addr          (addr_reg),
         .datain        (datain_reg),
         .rden          (1'b1),
